   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.2. Increased capacity of member States to ensure safe and healthy working conditions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. The Government of India published The Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code on 28 September 2020, as announced by the Ministry of Law and Justice (Legislative Department) in the Gazette of India. The scope of OSH protection, responsibilities of employers, duties of workers, and the mandate of labour inspectors, were clearly defined. 
2. Kerala constituted a State Tripartite Advisory Committee on Construction Safety in 2020. 
3. In the State of Rajasthan, the &#x2018;&#x2019;Rajasthan Policy on Pneumoconiosis including silicosis detection, prevention, control and rehabilitation&#x2019;&#x2019; adopted in September 2019 was implemented effectively. As of July 2021, 137 Health Screening Camps were organized and 3410 new silicosis cases were detected. With support from Government of Japan, the ILO:
1. Provided technical advice during discussions with Directorate General Factory Advice and Labour Institutes for developing the national OSH programme, identifying key areas for action. The ILO also provided expertise on OSH standards to state-level OSH policy discussions in the States of Kerala, Uttar Pradesh and Rajasthan for advancing the state OSH programme/sector-specific policies.
2. In Telengana, the ILO advocated stakeholders for improving understanding and creating an enabling environment for adopting the four pillars of FPRW in an integrated manner. More than 300 workshops/consultations organized with Government, Unions, CSOs, Research organizations at state, district, Mandal and village level to generate awareness and build capacity for implementation of FPRW. In addition, special campaigns on elimination of child labour were organized jointly with Govt. of Telangana through electronic &amp; print media, social media, involving key policy makers, actors and singers, reaching more than 900,000 people.
3. Advanced sector-specific OSH activities. For the small construction sector, the ILO drafted and published a new WISCON (Work Improvement for Small Construction Sites) training manual. A pilot training was held in January 2020 in State of Kerala, using the manual. For e-waste workers, a Hindi version of the OSH manual was developed and published in August 2021. A draft OSH flip book for stone-sector workers and employers in Rajasthan, and two draft OSH manuals for the metal and garment home-based workers were also prepared. 
4. Sub-regional OSH training programme for EBMO&#x2019;s Health and Hygiene Ambassadors (HHA) developed and 16 Indian employer participants were trained as HHAs. The ILO also provided webinars for preventing COVID-19 in workplaces. Following the webinars, participated workers and employers implemented concrete improvement in their respective workplaces and sent photos.</narrative>
